<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tongeren</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Atuatuca Tungrorum, Tongres</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tongeren is a town in the province of Limburg in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and claims to be the oldest town in the country. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atuatuca">Atuatuca</a> Tungrorum it was founded under Augustus as the chief town of the Tungri and grew into a walled Roman city on the road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavay">Bavay</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>. In the 4th century it was the see of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/servatius">Servatius</a>, from which the bishopric later moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maastricht">Maastricht</a> and then Liège.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tongeren.jpg" alt="Tongeren" width="320" height="174" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: original: Jeanhousen; derivative work by P199 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20100706_tongeren25-c.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tongeren lies in the southeastern corner of the Flemish region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, in the province of Limburg. It is called the oldest town in Belgium, as the only Roman administrative capital within the country's borders. Before the Roman conquest the area was inhabited by Belgic tribes known as the Germani cisrhenani, of which the Eburones were the largest. Caesar's Aduatuca, the fort of the Eburones, is widely equated with Tongeren, though others argue that Aduatuca was a general word for a fort and that Tongeren shows no sign of pre-Roman occupation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>As Atuatuca Tungrorum the town was the capital of the Civitas Tungrorum. Located on the road linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavay">Bavay</a> and surrounded by fertile land, it quickly became one of the largest Gallo-Roman administrative and military towns. It suffered a fire during the Batavian siege in 70 CE and in the second century built a defensive wall, parts of which survive. In 358 the future emperor Julian met a delegation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salian-franks">Salian Franks</a> there. In the 4th century it became the centre of a Christian diocese under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/servatius">Saint Servatius</a>, who died in 384, but the seat later moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maastricht">Maastricht</a> and then Liège. The town may have been destroyed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> in 451.</p><p>In Carolingian times a church and a chapter of canons were founded where the old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>' houses stood, and the present basilica was begun at the start of the 13th century. A medieval wall, new churches and the beguinage were built, and the town became one of the good cities of the Prince-Bishopric of Liège. Louis XIV's troops burned it almost entirely in 1677. It became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands</a> in 1815 and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> in 1830.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Béguinage, founded in 1257, has been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> since 1998, and the bell tower of the Basilica of Our Lady is inscribed with the Belfries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The Gallo-Roman Museum holds Celtic gold, Roman glassware and a Roman dodecahedron, and more than 1,500 metres of the second-century Roman wall can still be seen. A statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambiorix">Ambiorix</a>, leader of the Eburones, was erected in 1866. Every seven years the Kroningsfeesten, a religious procession, commemorates the crowning of the statue of Our Lady in 1890.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tongeren” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The bishopric that became Liège descended from the late Roman see of <b>Tongeren</b>, which moved to Maastricht and then, after the murder of Bishop Lambert at Liège around 705, to the place of his martyrdom, where his successor Hubert established the see in the early eighth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/prince-bishopric-of-liege">Prince-Bishopric of Liège · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Their chief town <b>Atuatuca Tungrorum</b> was founded under Augustus and grew into a walled city on the road from Bavay to Cologne.</blockquote><a href="/state/tungri">Tungri</a></li></ul></section>
